/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 6.5 University Review & Examination Questions) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Q1: Explain the NABC value proposition framework, articulating how each component) Tj
T*
(contributes to a compelling engineering business proposal.) Tj
T*
(Answer: Developed by the Stanford Research Institute, NABC structures proposals into) Tj
T*
(four vital pillars: \(1\) Need: Identifies a major, specific customer problem) Tj
T*
(substantiated by quantitative data and user research. \(2\) Approach: Details the) Tj
T*
(innovative technical and operational solution designed to address that need, supported) Tj
T*
(by prototype verification. \(3\) Benefits: Quantifies the tangible customer value per unit) Tj
T*
(cost \(efficiency gains, financial savings, safety enhancements\) delivered by the) Tj
T*
(solution. \(4\) Competition: Analyzes existing market alternatives, identifying unique) Tj
T*
(competitive barriers and explaining why current options or the status quo fail. NABC) Tj
T*
(ensures that engineering projects do not remain mere technical hobbies, but deliver) Tj
T*
(viable, scalable real-world impact.) Tj
T*
(* Q2: Explain the concept of a 'Product Pivot' and detail three distinct types of) Tj
T*
(pivots with real-world technological examples.) Tj
T*
(Answer: A Pivot is a structured course correction designed to test a new fundamental) Tj
T*
(hypothesis about a product, strategy, and engine of growth when user testing invalidates) Tj
T*
(initial assumptions. Three types include: \(1\) Feature Pivot: Elevating a single) Tj
T*
(successful sub-feature into the standalone product \(e.g., Flickr began as an online) Tj
T*
(multi-player video game where the photo-sharing chat tool became the entire company\).) Tj
T*
(\(2\) Customer Segment Pivot: Retaining the product but pivoting to a different market) Tj
T*
(cohort \(e.g., Slack began as an internal chat tool for game developers before pivoting) Tj
T*
(to enterprise business communication\). \(3\) Problem Pivot: Realizing through empathy that) Tj
T*
(the user's acute pain point is fundamentally different from the original design) Tj
T*
(assumption.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 6.6 Key Takeaways and Revision Points) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Testing is not the end of design; it informs refactoring, refinement, and pivots.) Tj
T*
(* Strategic pivots reframe products when core hypotheses fail user validation.) Tj
T*
(* The NABC model frames proposals through Need, Approach, Benefits, and Competition.) Tj
T*
(* An elevator pitch delivers a high-impact value proposition in under 90 seconds.) Tj
T*
ET

